.page sntp 1 ask an NTP server for the time, and set the clock

# SYNOPSIS
*sntp* [*-s*] [*-w*] [*-p* _PORT_] [*-t* _SECONDS_] _SERVER_
*ntpdate* [*-qw*] [*-p* _PORT_] [*-t* _SECONDS_] _SERVER_

# DESCRIPTION
`sntp` sends one simple NTP request (RFC 4330) to _SERVER_ and prints the stratum of the server, how far the local clock is from it (the offset, in seconds, positive when we are behind) and the round-trip delay. The offset is computed from the four timestamps of the exchange, so it does not include the time the packets spent travelling. The request is retried three times.

With `-s` the system clock is stepped by that offset (root only). The system clock keeps UTC; the time zone (`tz=` on the kernel command line, or `/etc/timezone`) only decides how the time is shown, so nothing about the zone needs to be given here. With `-w` the battery-backed clock chip is written as well, as `hwclock -w` would: in UTC, or in local time when the machine was started with `rtc=local` (a PC that also runs Windows does that).

`ntpdate` is the same program with `-s` implied: it asks and sets. Give it `-q` to only ask. A server that says it is not synchronised (stratum 0 or the alarm flag) is refused.

# OPTIONS
@ *-s*
    Step the system clock to the server's time.
@ *-w*
    Also write the clock chip (implies `-s`).
@ *-q*
    Only query, even for `ntpdate`.
@ *-p* _PORT_
    The server's UDP port (default 123).
@ *-t* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long for each answer (default 5).

# EXAMPLES
    sntp 10.0.2.2
    sntp -s ntp.example.test
    ntpdate -w 10.0.2.2

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when no valid answer came, the server is unsynchronised or the clock could not be set, 2 for a usage error.

# SEE ALSO
date(1), hwclock(8), clock(7), network(7)
